<given-names>A H</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff4">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Steel</surname><given-names>C M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Sir Alastair Currie CRC Laboratories, University of Edinburgh, Department of Pathology, Molecular Medicine Centre and <label>2</label>Department of Clinical Oncology, Western General Hospital, Crewe Road, Edinburgh, EH4 2XU, UK</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>3</label>School of Biomedical Sciences, Bute Medical Building, University of St Andrews, St Andrews, Fife, KY16 9TS, UK</aff><aff id="aff4"><label>4</label>Department of Pathology, University of Cambridge, Tennis Court Road, Cambridge, CB2 1QP, UK</aff><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>Author for correspondence: </corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>06</day><month>03</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>04</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><volume>82</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>1247</fpage><lpage>1248</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>01</day><month>06</month><year>1999</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>07</day><month>11</month><year>1999</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>22</day><month>11</month><year>1999</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 2000, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2000</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>The <italic>CYP19</italic> gene codes for the aromatase enzyme that is involved in the synthesis of oestrogens. This case&#x02013;control study examines the relationship between a tetranucleotide repeat sequence in the <italic>CYP19</italic> gene and the development of male breast cancer. No significant differences were found between male breast cancer cases and controls. &#x000a9; 2000 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>CYP19</kwd><kwd>male breast cancer</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>